"κ", "0.439", "91.2", "121", ".323"], ["Tier 2: healthcare access (6)", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["Usual care at a private clinic", "privateClinic", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.377", "80.3", "172", ".199"], ["Usual care at an HMO", "HMOClinic", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.422", "89.7", "172", ".208"], ["Usual care at a public clinic", "publicClinic", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.212", "93.7", "172", ".122"], ["Usual care at a hospital outpatient clinic", "outPatient", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.192", "91.5", "172", ".076"], ["Usual care at an emergency room", "emergencyCare", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.23", "80.5", "172", ".139"], ["Usual care at urgent care", "urgentCare", "Binary", "4062", "κ", "0.251", "77", "172", ".093"], ["Note. Predictors are grouped by tier and subdomain, with the number of predictors in parentheses; code is the variable name used in the analysis code and figures. Test-retest reliability is the Wave 1 to Wave 2 association among the n participants observed at both waves: Pearson r for continuous, Spearman ρ for ordinal, and Cohen's κ for binary and nominal predictors; agreement is the percentage of participants giving the same response at both waves. MZ co-twin resemblance is the same statistic computed between monozygotic co-twins at Wave 1. Summaries of test-retest reliability by measurement type and subdomain are in Table S12. Two configured variables, family history of heart attack (Tier 1) and weight loss by diet or exercise (Tier 2), were excluded before modeling for high missingness and are not listed. MZ = monozygotic.", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""], ["ᵃ Near zero because the predictor varies little among co-twins (at least 95% agreement, or at least 80% of values in one category); not evidence of absent resemblance.", "", "", "", "", "", "", "", ""]]}]}
